["<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Only 60 of the 5,000 People on a Carrier Fight; the Rest Restock Vending Machines On a U.S. carrier after 9/11, only fifty or sixty of the 5,000 people aboard ever touch a bomb. Everyone else spends the war restocking vending machines, doing laundry, and passing rumors — while the captain&#x27;s biggest worry is whether his sailors are watching the news. This American Life This American Life 206: Somewhere in the Arabian Sea U.S. military aircraft carrier daily life at war organizational management portraits of the young 9/11 blood chit Tailhook scandal Hollywood shower berthing XO / executive officer\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-24-tal-航母战争的日常-补零食-防谈恋爱-等回家/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/1da611bfe52eccd0-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:01:19</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">This American Life</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0824</span></div>\n<h2>Only 60 of the 5,000 People on a Carrier Fight; the Rest Restock Vending Machines</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">On a U.S. carrier after 9/11, only fifty or sixty of the 5,000 people aboard ever touch a bomb. Everyone else spends the war restocking vending machines, doing laundry, and passing rumors — while the captain&#x27;s biggest worry is whether his sailors are watching the news.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">U.S. military</span><span class=\"tag\">aircraft carrier</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Screens train multitasking, not learning: the harder classrooms digitize, the worse kids do The harder classrooms have pushed digital tools since 2010, the further children&#x27;s cognition and digital literacy have fallen across the board; screens train multitasking rather than learning, and AI only makes the errors harder to see. Intelligence Squared Intelligence Squared Is Tech Making Our Kids Less Capable? With Dr Jared Cooney Horvath Ed tech Classroom screens Cognitive development AI in education Digital literacy Contrarian Ed tech ICILS Offloading Outsourcing SRLs Drill-and-kill\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-iq2-技术不等于学习-屏幕正在让一代人变笨/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/46f8e9d9c37f6c72-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">33:14</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Intelligence Squared</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>Screens train multitasking, not learning: the harder classrooms digitize, the worse kids do</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The harder classrooms have pushed digital tools since 2010, the further children&#x27;s cognition and digital literacy have fallen across the board; screens train multitasking rather than learning, and AI only makes the errors harder to see.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Ed tech</span><span class=\"tag\">Classroom screens</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"British misjudgement lit the fuse in Northern Ireland: the Provisional IRA meant to fight all along A run of British government misjudgements in the early 1970s — the Falls Curfew, the refusal to send more troops, indiscriminate internment — turned the Provisional IRA from a fringe armed group into the central actor of a thirty-year conflict, and radicalised British soldiers along the way. The Rest Is History The Rest Is History 699. The Troubles: Return of the IRA (Part 3) The Troubles IRA Counter-insurgency British politics Insurgency Sectarian violence The Troubles Operation Demetrius UVF\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-restishistory-临时ira绝非防御组织-英军误判铸成三十年冲突/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4d082c6220b28920-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:23:51</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Rest Is History</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>British misjudgement lit the fuse in Northern Ireland: the Provisional IRA meant to fight all along</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">A run of British government misjudgements in the early 1970s — the Falls Curfew, the refusal to send more troops, indiscriminate internment — turned the Provisional IRA from a fringe armed group into the central actor of a thirty-year conflict, and radicalised British soldiers along the way.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">The Troubles</span><span class=\"tag\">IRA</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Mental events follow no laws, yet reasons can still be causes that leave us free Davidson argues that mental events fall under no strict laws and are identical to physical events only as particulars; but in the explanation of action, reasons are genuinely causes, and when a reason causes an action the agent is precisely then free. The Partially Examined Life Partially Examined Life Ep. 398: Donald Davidson on Mind vs. Body (Part Two) Mind-body problem Philosophy of action Anomalous monism Causal explanation Davidson Analytic philosophy token identity homonomic generalization heteronomic generalization pro attitude primary reason rationalization\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-pel-心物无规律-理由仍可作为原因而自由/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4024c3bbaf655b13-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">53:45</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Partially Examined Life</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>Mental events follow no laws, yet reasons can still be causes that leave us free</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Davidson argues that mental events fall under no strict laws and are identical to physical events only as particulars; but in the explanation of action, reasons are genuinely causes, and when a reason causes an action the agent is precisely then free.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Mind-body problem</span><span class=\"tag\">Philosophy of action</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\" data-hay=\"A 22-Year-Old CEO, Five Rounds, Over $100 Million: The First Term Sheet Decides Everything In a year and a half, 22-year-old Huang Yi has grown RoboParty to nearly 150 people: five rounds totalling over $100 million, and RPO shipping around 100 units a month. His calls: the first round&#x27;s terms decide whether you live or die, open source is a moat because it lowers the friction of working with you, and the robot body is the entry point to embodied AI. 十字路口Crossing 十字路口Crossing 22 岁的具身 CEO、5 轮融资、过亿美元、“不知天有多高”、“一年吃了十年的苦”｜对谈黄一：萝博派对创始人/CEO Embodied AI Robotics Fundraising Open Source Startup Management Org Design missing layer deep pocket BFM (behavior foundation model) sim2real honeycomb structure\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-crossing-具身创业最优解-快融资-软条款-全栈开源/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/3434775ccb19b8d7-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"十\"><span class=\"dur\">1:02:53</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">十字路口Crossing</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>A 22-Year-Old CEO, Five Rounds, Over $100 Million: The First Term Sheet Decides Everything</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In a year and a half, 22-year-old Huang Yi has grown RoboParty to nearly 150 people: five rounds totalling over $100 million, and RPO shipping around 100 units a month. His calls: the first round&#x27;s terms decide whether you live or die, open source is a moat because it lowers the friction of working with you, and the robot body is the entry point to embodied AI.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>2</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Embodied AI</span><span class=\"tag\">Robotics Fundraising</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"What Etched&#x27;s chip really solves may be bandwidth, not compute Etched welds the transformer into hardware with 0.45-volt ultra-low-voltage transistors and direct cross-chip SRAM access, but the host suspects the problem actually being solved is bandwidth, not compute. TechTechPotato TechTechPotato The Reason My Phone is Ringing Daily: Etched AI chips Transformer-specific chips Compute efficiency Funding Bandwidth bottleneck tape out RDMA threshold voltage cluster scale memory\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-techtechpotato-etched的芯片-可能解决的是带宽而不是算力/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/5ab75e84ff855e23-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">TechTechPotato</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>What Etched&#x27;s chip really solves may be bandwidth, not compute</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Etched welds the transformer into hardware with 0.45-volt ultra-low-voltage transistors and direct cross-chip SRAM access, but the host suspects the problem actually being solved is bandwidth, not compute.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI chips</span><span class=\"tag\">Transformer-specific chips</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"The moat in evals is not the AI — it is human taste You cannot leave eval design to the AI: it is good at top-down criteria, but the bottom-up evals that actually decide whether a product is good — the taste distilled out of error data — have to come from a human, and only then get executed at scale with Claude Code. Peter Yang Peter Yang How to Build Better AI Evals with Claude Code in 5 Steps | Shreya &amp; Hamel Eval systems Claude Code Error analysis LLM products Human review bottom-up evals top-down evals LLM judge error discovery staccato fragments slop\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-peteryang-自下而上的评估-是-ai-给不了你的护城河/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/49510d80b4317525-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"P\"><span class=\"dur\">54:20</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Peter Yang</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>The moat in evals is not the AI — it is human taste</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">You cannot leave eval design to the AI: it is good at top-down criteria, but the bottom-up evals that actually decide whether a product is good — the taste distilled out of error data — have to come from a human, and only then get executed at scale with Claude Code.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Eval systems</span><span class=\"tag\">Claude Code</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Ebola infects neurons and keeps shedding virus for 120 days; a fish cancer spreads between fish Brain organoids show that Ebola can infect neurons and keep releasing infectious virus for 120 days; melanomas in a brown bullhead population descend from a single clonal ancestor and spread from fish to fish. The boundaries of viral persistence and of transmissible cancer get rewritten at the same time. This Week in Virology This Week in Virology TWiV 1351: Ebola Virus Brains and Contagious Catfish Cancers Viral persistence Brain organoids Ebola Transmissible cancer NIH funding Biosafety productive persistence nonproductive persistence BSL-4 MOI\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-twiv-埃博拉持续感染脑神经元-鱼类黑色素瘤能传染/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><div class=\"fallback\">T</div><span class=\"dur\">1:41:09</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">This Week in Virology</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>Ebola infects neurons and keeps shedding virus for 120 days; a fish cancer spreads between fish</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Brain organoids show that Ebola can infect neurons and keep releasing infectious virus for 120 days; melanomas in a brown bullhead population descend from a single clonal ancestor and spread from fish to fish. The boundaries of viral persistence and of transmissible cancer get rewritten at the same time.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Viral persistence</span><span class=\"tag\">Brain organoids</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Terror birds weren&#x27;t killed by humans — the Isthmus of Panama closed first Terror birds were the apex avian predators that emerged on the island continent of South America after the dinosaurs died out, and they ruled for tens of millions of years; their eventual disappearance is tied to the great species interchange that followed the joining of the Isthmus of Panama, earlier than the arrival of humans in South America. The Ancients The Ancients Terror Birds Paleontology Bird evolution South America Extinction Asteroid impact Seriemas Eocene Isthmus of Panama\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-23-ancients-恐怖鸟死于物种大交换而非人类/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/270440780adbe49b-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">45:14</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The Ancients</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0823</span></div>\n<h2>Terror birds weren&#x27;t killed by humans — the Isthmus of Panama closed first</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Terror birds were the apex avian predators that emerged on the island continent of South America after the dinosaurs died out, and they ruled for tens of millions of years; their eventual disappearance is tied to the great species interchange that followed the joining of the Isthmus of Panama, earlier than the arrival of humans in South America.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Paleontology</span><span class=\"tag\">Bird evolution</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Ukrainians Are Fighting the War With Poems and Artifacts, Not Weapons How the war turned a culture reporter into a language student and prose writers into poets: Ukrainians are resisting the war&#x27;s erasure of identity and memory by rescuing artifacts and writing poems. Intelligence Squared Intelligence Squared Is Ukraine’s Culture Worth Fighting For? With Charlotte Higgins Ukraine Culture and war Poetry Museums Identity War narrative Cyrillic alphabet Lviv Book Forum citizen army\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-22-iq2-在乌克兰-文化是值得用生命去救的东西/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/0eefaf93ef4770ba-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">37:37</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Intelligence Squared</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0822</span></div>\n<h2>Ukrainians Are Fighting the War With Poems and Artifacts, Not Weapons</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">How the war turned a culture reporter into a language student and prose writers into poets: Ukrainians are resisting the war&#x27;s erasure of identity and memory by rescuing artifacts and writing poems.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Ukraine</span><span class=\"tag\">Culture and war</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Encrypted Reasoning Is Not a Secret: A Small Model Will Read the Big One&#x27;s Thinking Out Loud Frontier models hand the user an encrypted copy of their thinking along with the answer, but that ciphertext can be replayed verbatim into a smaller model from the same family, which will simply read it out — opening the door to privacy leaks, jailbreaks, prompt injection, and even a channel for distilling capability. Machine Learning Street Talk ML Street Talk Stealing Reasoning Traces from Proprietary LLM APIs — Ilia Shumailov &amp; Alexander Panfilov Reasoning encryption Jailbreaks Model distillation AI security Prompt injection Privacy leaks reasoning trace prefill replay attack responsible disclosure\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-22-mlst-加密思考不是秘密-小模型能把大模型的推理念出来/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7306f7dff4dc8ceb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"M\"><span class=\"dur\">49:00</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Machine Learning Street Talk</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0822</span></div>\n<h2>Encrypted Reasoning Is Not a Secret: A Small Model Will Read the Big One&#x27;s Thinking Out Loud</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Frontier models hand the user an encrypted copy of their thinking along with the answer, but that ciphertext can be replayed verbatim into a smaller model from the same family, which will simply read it out — opening the door to privacy leaks, jailbreaks, prompt injection, and even a channel for distilling capability.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Reasoning encryption</span><span class=\"tag\">Jailbreaks</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"The people who find AI incidents are never the safety evaluators In every agent-gone-wrong incident this summer, the ones who noticed first were infrastructure security teams — researchers running evals account for precisely zero cases; and to keep pace with attacks, defenders are being forced to hand more permissions to the very agents just shown to cross the line. The Cognitive Revolution Cognitive Revolution AI in the AM — Weekly Highlights: Relaunch Week (Aug 17–20, 2026) safety evals agent incidents inference cost open weights compute AI governance CoBench mind virus ghostjacking effective compute consumption Minimal Standard for Safeguards\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-22-cogrev-ai-事故的发现者从来不是安全评估员/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/dd01d2cd4bc88e5a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">2:33:41</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The Cognitive Revolution</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0822</span></div>\n<h2>The people who find AI incidents are never the safety evaluators</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In every agent-gone-wrong incident this summer, the ones who noticed first were infrastructure security teams — researchers running evals account for precisely zero cases; and to keep pace with attacks, defenders are being forced to hand more permissions to the very agents just shown to cross the line.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">safety evals</span><span class=\"tag\">agent incidents</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Food Safety Standards Born of Space Food: Testing the Finished Product Is Never Enough HACCP, the core of the global food safety system, was born out of NASA&#x27;s space food program: testing the finished product is never enough, and the whole process from ingredient to person has to be controlled; a 1971 botulism case and the 1993 Jack in the Box outbreak turned it into a mandatory global standard. Stuff You Missed in History Class Stuff You Missed in History Class SYMHC Classics: Astronaut Origins of Food Safety Food Safety HACCP NASA Regulation Public Health Food Industry HACCP / Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points CCP / Critical Control Point Codex Alimentarius FMEA / Failure Mode and Effects Analysis\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-22-stuffmissed-haccp-为防止宇航员食物中毒而生的全球食安标准/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/88586eb98e196b1a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">36:42</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Stuff You Missed in History Class</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0822</span></div>\n<h2>Food Safety Standards Born of Space Food: Testing the Finished Product Is Never Enough</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">HACCP, the core of the global food safety system, was born out of NASA&#x27;s space food program: testing the finished product is never enough, and the whole process from ingredient to person has to be controlled; a 1971 botulism case and the 1993 Jack in the Box outbreak turned it into a mandatory global standard.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Food Safety</span><span class=\"tag\">HACCP</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"A question is defined by the act of seeking information, not the question mark A real question is defined by its function, not its form; asking is a power you can practise — and most of the answers AI hands out today have not answered any question that was seriously posed. Philosophy Bites Philosophy Bites Lani Watson on the Power of Questions Questioning Philosophy Education AI criticism Communication Socrates information seeking act rhetorical question private questions and social questions answer oriented model\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-22-phibites-真正的问题不是问号-而是寻求信息的行动/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/243c92b44ec40dbb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"P\"><span class=\"dur\">16:28</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Philosophy Bites</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0822</span></div>\n<h2>A question is defined by the act of seeking information, not the question mark</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">A real question is defined by its function, not its form; asking is a power you can practise — and most of the answers AI hands out today have not answered any question that was seriously posed.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Questioning</span><span class=\"tag\">Philosophy</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"A 20-Person Team Burned $2 Billion: AI Rewrites the Ceiling on Capital Efficiency AI lets a 20-person team spend $2 billion efficiently, and venture capital shifts from zero-sum competition to strategic control points; the frontier labs&#x27; funding advantage will eventually fade, and the application layer captures more of the value. The a16z Show a16z Martin Casado on Where the Value Is Going in AI AI investing Capital efficiency Model competition Open source M&amp;A Startups autocatalytic effects AI complete token path two-sided marketplace founder-market fit\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-22-a16z-小团队吞下亿级资本-ai-改写投资逻辑/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7b2f032532c3a865-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">42:01</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The a16z Show</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0822</span></div>\n<h2>A 20-Person Team Burned $2 Billion: AI Rewrites the Ceiling on Capital Efficiency</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">AI lets a 20-person team spend $2 billion efficiently, and venture capital shifts from zero-sum competition to strategic control points; the frontier labs&#x27; funding advantage will eventually fade, and the application layer captures more of the value.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI investing</span><span class=\"tag\">Capital efficiency</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Giving Tamiflu too late in critical flu doubles the risk of death Critically ill flu patients who start Tamiflu only about five days into symptoms face roughly double the 90-day risk of death compared with no antiviral; an mRNA flu vaccine is approved, but only 50-64-year-olds got traditional approval. This Week in Virology This Week in Virology TWiV 1350: Clinical update with Dr. Daniel Griffin Influenza RSV mRNA vaccines Vaccine hesitancy Tamiflu Public health cyclosporiasis New World screwworm nirsevimab test-negative case-control adaptive analysis non-medical exemption\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-22-twiv-重症流感太晚用达菲-可能反而提高死亡风险/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><div class=\"fallback\">T</div><span class=\"dur\">1:01:12</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">This Week in Virology</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0822</span></div>\n<h2>Giving Tamiflu too late in critical flu doubles the risk of death</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Critically ill flu patients who start Tamiflu only about five days into symptoms face roughly double the 90-day risk of death compared with no antiviral; an mRNA flu vaccine is approved, but only 50-64-year-olds got traditional approval.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Influenza</span><span class=\"tag\">RSV</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\" data-hay=\"Western Restaurant Brands Go Chinese-Owned; The Real Moat Is In Fragrance Compounds McDonald&#x27;s, Starbucks, Burger King and their peers have handed their China businesses to Chinese capital one after another. In restaurants, localisation is the line between life and death — but the fragrance compounds upstream of perfume are still monopolised by four European and American giants. It is the barrier inside the supply chain, not the brand on the door, that decides where domestic substitution actually stops. 疯投圈 疯投圈 145 ✪ 改属中资的餐饮洋品牌 Restaurants Localisation Foreign capital retreat Perfume &amp; fragrance Starbucks Domestic substitution CFB (China Food &amp; Beverage Group)\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-22-fengtouquan-洋餐饮纷纷让位中资-硬壁垒藏在香气里/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/f4193533f4aa66ed-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"疯\"><span class=\"dur\">1:12:11</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">疯投圈</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0822</span></div>\n<h2>Western Restaurant Brands Go Chinese-Owned; The Real Moat Is In Fragrance Compounds</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">McDonald&#x27;s, Starbucks, Burger King and their peers have handed their China businesses to Chinese capital one after another. In restaurants, localisation is the line between life and death — but the fragrance compounds upstream of perfume are still monopolised by four European and American giants. It is the barrier inside the supply chain, not the brand on the door, that decides where domestic substitution actually stops.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Restaurants</span><span class=\"tag\">Localisation</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Simulating real people is not a reasoning problem: frontier models get only 20–30% accuracy Joon Sung Park&#x27;s argument: treating people as trainable objects does not call for a stronger reasoning model but for a model that makes the same mistakes people make — frontier models reach only 20–30% accuracy predicting the behavior of niche populations, and Simile has pushed that to 85% using two-hour deep interviews plus RCT data. Latent Space Latent Space Simulation: the new Scaling Law — Joon Sung Park, Simile AI Simulation Post-training Behavioral data Synthetic panels Scaling law Market research social physics attitudinal vs. behavioral concept testing synthetic panel agent-based modeling Open Science Framework pre-registration\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-21-latentspace-前沿模型太聪明-反而模拟不了真人/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/bb52be8f143fe3c1-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"L\"><span class=\"dur\">1:09:38</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Latent Space</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0821</span></div>\n<h2>Simulating real people is not a reasoning problem: frontier models get only 20–30% accuracy</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Joon Sung Park&#x27;s argument: treating people as trainable objects does not call for a stronger reasoning model but for a model that makes the same mistakes people make — frontier models reach only 20–30% accuracy predicting the behavior of niche populations, and Simile has pushed that to 85% using two-hour deep interviews plus RCT data.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Simulation</span><span class=\"tag\">Post-training</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Being Itself Has No Content: Peirce Overturns Substance-Centrism in Nine Pages In a nine-page paper from 1868, Peirce takes ‘a concept must be able to unify the content of consciousness’ as his test, deriving being from substance and then the intermediate categories such as quality. Being itself has no content; it only connects a predicate to a subject. The Partially Examined Life Partially Examined Life PEL Presents Closereads: Peirce&#x27;s New Categories (Part One) Philosophy Categories Peirce Transcendental Deduction Abstraction Unity manifold precision ground copula substance\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-21-pel-peirce的范畴论-存在无内容-实体是当下的它/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/1bb9773e6a8da6d8-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:01:06</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Partially Examined Life</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0821</span></div>\n<h2>Being Itself Has No Content: Peirce Overturns Substance-Centrism in Nine Pages</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In a nine-page paper from 1868, Peirce takes ‘a concept must be able to unify the content of consciousness’ as his test, deriving being from substance and then the intermediate categories such as quality. Being itself has no content; it only connects a predicate to a subject.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>2</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Philosophy</span><span class=\"tag\">Categories</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"The open-source ban is coming: stand up the body, write it into law, then close open source in the name of fairness Sacks lays out the three steps by which an open-source ban actually lands: first stand up the body, then write it into law, then shut open source down in the name of standards applying equally. Chamath argues the real political fuel is voters&#x27; revulsion at two men about to become trillionaires, and has nothing to do with AI safety or with water and power. All-In Podcast All-In Dario Defends Himself, Datacenter Panic, AI Doomer Trap, Senate Toss-Up Regulatory capture Open models Data centers harness Midterms harness SRO (self-regulatory organization) RSI (recursive self-improvement) interlocking directorates DSA (Democratic Socialists of America)\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-21-allin-反数据中心浪潮的燃料不是缺水缺电-是万亿富翁/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/0d37432d346f02be-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">1:30:53</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">All-In Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0821</span></div>\n<h2>The open-source ban is coming: stand up the body, write it into law, then close open source in the name of fairness</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Sacks lays out the three steps by which an open-source ban actually lands: first stand up the body, then write it into law, then shut open source down in the name of standards applying equally. Chamath argues the real political fuel is voters&#x27; revulsion at two men about to become trillionaires, and has nothing to do with AI safety or with water and power.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Regulatory capture</span><span class=\"tag\">Open models</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Patient zero is not the source, it is the scapegoat society needs From Typhoid Mary to HIV to the high five, every so-called origin is a narrative humans need; the truth is a jigsaw, and all we want is the focal point. Radiolab Radiolab Patient Zero Patient Zero Typhoid Mary HIV Origins High Five Narrative Building Public Health healthy carrier spillover molecular clock cut hunter SIV\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-21-radiolab-零号病人不是起点-而是被选中的人/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/66ac5d70f1e2077a-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"R\"><span class=\"dur\">51:33</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Radiolab</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0821</span></div>\n<h2>Patient zero is not the source, it is the scapegoat society needs</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">From Typhoid Mary to HIV to the high five, every so-called origin is a narrative humans need; the truth is a jigsaw, and all we want is the focal point.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Patient Zero</span><span class=\"tag\">Typhoid Mary</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Replacing the Missing Orexin: Narcolepsy Gets a Drug That Treats the Cause, and Maybe a GLP-1 for the Brain The FDA has approved the first orexin agonist, which replaces the orexin that type 1 narcolepsy patients lack instead of merely managing symptoms; it could become a ‘GLP-1 for the brain’. Separately, brain organoids have survived past five years for the first time, opening up research into late-onset brain disease. Nature Podcast Nature Podcast Briefing Chat: New narcolepsy drug could unlock host of novel brain therapies Narcolepsy Orexin GLP-1 analogy Brain organoids FDA approval Neuro drugs narcolepsy cataplexy orexin orexin agonist brain organoid GLP1\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-21-naturepod-缺失的食欲素被补上-发作性睡病有了治本药/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/52b8ecfabe6e81c0-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"N\"><span class=\"dur\">12:25</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Nature Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0821</span></div>\n<h2>Replacing the Missing Orexin: Narcolepsy Gets a Drug That Treats the Cause, and Maybe a GLP-1 for the Brain</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The FDA has approved the first orexin agonist, which replaces the orexin that type 1 narcolepsy patients lack instead of merely managing symptoms; it could become a ‘GLP-1 for the brain’. Separately, brain organoids have survived past five years for the first time, opening up research into late-onset brain disease.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Narcolepsy</span><span class=\"tag\">Orexin</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\" data-hay=\"A junior-high dropout who can&#x27;t draw won an Apple Design Award A self-taught outsider with a junior-high education who cannot draw built the first non-game app from China to win an Apple Design Award — by running into the wall again and again and never letting a feeling of ‘something&#x27;s off’ slide. Taste is not a gift; it is a habit built by long training. 知行小酒馆 知行小酒馆 E248 从 QQ 空间到苹果设计大奖：一个非典型设计师的成长之路 Design Taste Product thinking Apple Design Award AI products Personal growth Flashcard Feature phone Quality\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-21-zhixing-没得选的野路子-如何撞出苹果设计大奖/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/f7a02aabb852635f-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"知\"><span class=\"dur\">1:18:49</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">知行小酒馆</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0821</span></div>\n<h2>A junior-high dropout who can&#x27;t draw won an Apple Design Award</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">A self-taught outsider with a junior-high education who cannot draw built the first non-game app from China to win an Apple Design Award — by running into the wall again and again and never letting a feeling of ‘something&#x27;s off’ slide. Taste is not a gift; it is a habit built by long training.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Design</span><span class=\"tag\">Taste</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"edu\" data-hay=\"Small Model Directs Big Model: AI Scientist Outperforms Claude in Reproducing Papers Fine-tuning a small model to call a stronger model as a tool proved more reliable than Claude and Codex at reproducing paper results—thanks to weighted reward allocation and weighting tool calls. Hugging Face Hugging Face Hugging Face Journal Club: Training AI Scientists to Replicate Research Reinforcement Learning AI Scientist RLHF Tool Use Paper Reproduction Infrastructure rubric judge reward hacking GRPO ring attention\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-21-huggingface-小模型指挥大模型-ai-科学家复现论文超过-claude/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/f339a70c3ef285af-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"H\"><span class=\"dur\">35:03</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"edu\"><span class=\"src\">Hugging Face</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0821</span></div>\n<h2>Small Model Directs Big Model: AI Scientist Outperforms Claude in Reproducing Papers</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Fine-tuning a small model to call a stronger model as a tool proved more reliable than Claude and Codex at reproducing paper results—thanks to weighted reward allocation and weighting tool calls.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>4</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Reinforcement Learning</span><span class=\"tag\">AI Scientist</span>\n</div></div></a>"]